<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savitsky Museum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as State Museum of Arts of the Republic of Karakalpakstan, Nukus Museum of Art</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Savitsky Museum, the State Museum of Arts of Karakalpakstan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukus">Nukus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, was founded in 1966 by the artist and collector Igor Savitsky. Far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, he gathered tens of thousands of works, including Russian and Central Asian avant-garde paintings banned under Stalin, together with Karakalpak folk art. It holds one of the largest collections of Soviet avant-garde art in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savitsky-museum.jpg" alt="Savitsky Museum" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ayzadarich · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Karakalpakstan_State_Museum_of_Art_named_after_I.V._Savitsky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
